00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.730 align:start position:0%
 
uh very good morning to you Mick Lynch  thanks so much for talking to us this  morning first of all I wanted you to  give us an update on the talks last  night as I understand at the rail  Minister Hugh Merriman was there in  network Rail and the rail delivery group  was any progress made  yes the Royal Minister convene those  talks so we attended as invited as with  the other parties we had an exchange  about what might be possible and uh away  you know some ways forward and ideas  that all the parties shared and the the  rail Minister requested that all the  parties get down to some more  discussions uh in the in the next period  we'll look to arrange those meetings  with the employers and see if we can  develop some solutions to the to the  issues that hopefully all the parties  can support but there were no actual  negotiations there were some soundings  out of what might might be developed so  we'll look forward to getting around the  table with the employers uh and work it  up and see what we can do but there is  no actual there are no new proposals on  the table as we speak  tight amongst the workers themselves to  strike a deal this after the tssa union  accepted Network rails uh pay offer  you've rejected that deal one that the  tssa has accepted why have you rejected  when they think it's adequate  well the tssa and United are minuscule  in network rail we've got eight or nine  times their membership and they're not  affected by the changes that Network  rail which is to make they're generally  not out in the track in all weathers  they're not actually doing the work they  are supervisors and why collar workers  they don't do the work that our members  do who are profoundly affected by the  changes including the safety regime as  will be the public so it's a completely  different set of workers for them it's  essentially a clean no strings deal  there's some minor amendments to their  their work life balance and so on but  ours our members have got completely  different set of terms and conditions  inherited from the private sector which  doesn't really affect them so whether  they're in the in the dispute or not  doesn't really affect the course of  dispute and for our members the the  proposals are completely inadequate at  this time but we'll keep working with  network rail and the talks in order to  get some solutions and that's what we're  aiming to do over the next period so as  you say a different situation for  different unions but does it make your  job harder when other unions accept the  same offer  well they're independent unions they're  I mean I'm not involved in those unions  it's up to them they make their  decisions if they want to get out of the  dispute and accept what's what's on the  table for them that's up to them but the  offer for us is a different offer they  have a different collective bargaining  arrangement for some of their members  and as I say they're not affected by  these changes it doesn't really affect  our members whether the tssa are in this  or not these are the people that  supervise our members they're not really  the people that are affected by the  changes so we'll keep working it up on  behalf of our people it's often away on  the railway that the rmt has to lead  and others follow or drop out of the  disputes at early stages that's been the  way for  a century and a half so far so we'll  probably continue into the future  this is the start of the second 48-hour  strike this week more planned next week  in the run-up to Christmas so what would  it take now to call off off those  strikes next week  all resolutions to disputes are about  compromises we understand what the  companies want and they understand what  what we need so we need some compromise  on some of the conditions they're  putting on the offer and we'll need an  improvement in the payoffer that is  achievable in my view and in the view of  my negotiating team are my executive  committee I also think it's probably the  view of the company so we need the the  government to facilitate that and use  their good offices to get a deal I'll be  working on documents and papers with the  companies if that's what they want to do  with me and then we'll try and get a  solution but there is there is an  agreement there to be had and we'll keep  working on that and try and get to a  position where we can put those  proposals to our members and ultimately  our members will decide whether the two  sets of proposals are acceptable to them  or not  you say there's agreement there to be  had are you feeling any more optimistic  I'm always optimistic I have to be  optimistic in order to get a deal I know  that there are some very simple steps  that the employers and ourselves could  take together to get a solution to this  and that means a common sense approach  uh both sides get into a position where  there's a some commonly held uh  positions and I think we can do that in  the next period And if that is done very  quickly we can consider the industrial  action going forward and of course if we  get a set of documentation and a pay  proposal that our members want to  support it will resolve the dispute and  we can take all of the action away I  hope we can do that as quickly as  possible  a lot of people worried about those  strikes next week they're worried about  how to impact their Christmas plans can  you give us any specifics about what it  will take to call off those strikes next  week  well we'll deal with that with the  government the strikes next week as it  start on Christmas Eve during the wind  down of the Railway uh are about the  engineering works at Network rail are  carrying out when the railway is closed  so there's a full week now between now  and that period if Network rail want to  work with us on some solutions we can  consider that but you know there are  some very straightforward things that  they can do some very straightforward  things they can do to the pay proposals  and to the changes that they want and  they're all getting to deep discussions  with them if that's what it takes and  the minister clearly asked us to do that  in the next period And if we can get  some solutions I can't negotiate with  Skye on the television I've got to do  that in a room with the employers  because that's where meaningful talks  happen and some of that stuff is quite  technical it's about rostering it's  about the way that the people are  deployed it's about their skill sets  it's about safety on the railway so  we'll do that with them and if they can  come towards us a little bit we can  maybe work up some solutions  are you worried though that action at  Christmas will lose you public support  well the public is with us we I get lots  of messages of support we get support  when we're out on our picket lines we  know that the public will be upset and  even angry about the disruption some of  that anger should be put towards the  government and the companies we believe  but the disruption for people on these  strike days that are happening actually  happening at Christmas will be minimal  the railway shuts down on Christmas Eve  in any case to do engineering works  there aren't scheduled trains on  Christmas day nor on boxing day and the  railway curtails its activities early on  Christmas Eve that will be a little bit  earlier than usual but people have got  time now to make plans and I hope that  they're successful in that and that we  can progress these talks to maybe get  some Solutions in the next week or so

